mock-data-generator/node_modules/*
sample/*
.vscode/*
mock-data-generator/markers/*
batch-app/markers/*
/.idea/
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo test-suite generated outputs, produced by scripts/build_test_*.sh and
# scripts/run_*.sh.
# Assumptions: this file names only GENERATED artifacts, so no rule in it can mask a
#   real source file. That narrowness is the policy for every block below too.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiled COBOL programs and shared modules (CARDDEMO_BUILD_DIR default).
/build/
# JUnit XML and coverage reports (CARDDEMO_REPORTS_DIR default).
/reports/
# gcov artifacts from the GnuCOBOL-transpiled C (--coverage build).
# Assumptions: kept global rather than scoped to /build/, so a manual gcov run in a
#   source tree is covered too. The mutable per-run test workspace and its loaded VSAM
#   index files need no rule at all: scripts/test_env.sh creates them under $TMPDIR,
#   outside the repository.
*.gcno
*.gcda
*.gcov
# Python-harness coverage data (coverage.py; parallel mode writes .coverage.*).
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
# pytest and Python bytecode caches. Unanchored, so they also cover data-migration/.
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo AWS migration -- generated build output, IaC state, local env.
# The migration adds four build trees (services/, ui/, data-migration/, infra/) whose
# toolchains all write generated bytes back into the working tree.
# Assumptions: this set is intentionally NARROW, naming only generated artifacts and
# secret-bearing local files, so it cannot mask a real source file. The state and env
# rules carry the extra weight, because Terraform state records the resolved value of
# every attribute it manages -- including any marked sensitive -- in cleartext, and
# nothing encrypts it inside the working tree.
#
# Two kinds of value reach state here, and neither is a credential:
# (1) Never in state, by construction. The per-service database credentials come from an
# `ephemeral "random_password"` written through `secret_string_wo`, so the provider
# receives them and Terraform records only a version number. The Aurora master credential
# is created and held by RDS under `manage_master_user_password`. The Cognito seed-user
# passwords are produced out of band and written straight into Secrets Manager. And no
# TLS private key reaches state either: no `tls_private_key` resource and no `tls`
# provider requirement remains in `infra/`, because each task mints its own listener
# material at startup instead of being handed a managed PEM.
# (2) Not secret but not harmless: every resource identifier, ARN, endpoint and network
# address in the environment. State and a saved plan carry all of them, and together they
# are a map of the deployment -- which is why the rules below refuse them regardless.
#
# Refactoring Rationale: this block used to name an imported HTTPS private key, and then a
# GENERATED one, as the concrete state-bearing value. Both are stale: the two PEM inputs
# were withdrawn from both roots, and the generating resource was withdrawn after them, so
# there is no key material in state to warn about. Leaving either wording would have sent a
# reader looking for a variable or a resource that no longer exists.
#
# Assumptions: what these rules can carry, stated so none is mistaken for a control it
#   is not. An ignore rule keeps a path out of `git status` and out of an unqualified
#   `git add`, which removes the likeliest route to a committed state file: staging a
#   directory without reading it. It does not make committing one impossible -- `git
#   add -f` overrides any rule, and a rule added later does not govern an
#   ALREADY-TRACKED path. Accidental-staging protection is the whole of it.
# Trade-offs: the hard gate therefore lives elsewhere and cannot live here. The
#   no-secrets-in-the-repository constraint is met primarily by generating every
#   credential at apply time straight into Secrets Manager, by keeping the
#   per-environment tfvars limited to sizing and retention values, and by having
#   deployment authenticate through short-lived federated role assumption; the
#   remaining pre-merge gate is the gitleaks scan in
#   .github/workflows/infra-ci.yml, which inspects CONTENT and which `git add -f`
#   cannot bypass.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Maven build output, one target/ per module under services/ plus one at the
# aggregator, where the Checkstyle gate bound to `validate` writes its result files.
# Assumptions: deliberately NOT root-anchored, unlike /build/ and /reports/ above,
#   because every target/ is nested under services/ and never at the repository root,
#   where a leading slash would match nothing.
target/

# npm dependency tree installed for the React SPA under ui/.
# Assumptions: the legacy `mock-data-generator/node_modules/*` rule at the top of this
#   file is PATH-SCOPED to a tree this repository no longer carries, so this unanchored
#   rule is required rather than redundant.
node_modules/

# Vite production bundle, written by `npm run build` to ui/dist/. Unanchored for the
# same reason as target/ above.
dist/

# Provider and module cache created by `terraform init`.
# Alternatives Considered: ignoring all of infra/. Rejected outright -- the .tf source
#   under infra/ IS the deliverable, so a blanket rule would mask the very files the
#   migration exists to ship.
# Assumptions: the trailing slash matches the cache DIRECTORY only, which is what keeps
#   the sibling .terraform.lock.hcl tracked. That file pins provider checksums and is
#   what makes `terraform init` reproducible, so the slash is load-bearing.
.terraform/

# Terraform state, plus every backup and workspace-suffixed variant.
# Trade-offs: state records the resolved value of every attribute it manages, including
#   any marked sensitive, in cleartext, and nothing encrypts it inside the working tree.
#   This stack keeps its generated credentials outside that exposure -- per-service
#   secrets are written through `secret_string_wo`, the Aurora master credential is held
#   by RDS under `manage_master_user_password`, and Cognito seed-user passwords are
#   written straight into Secrets Manager -- so what state discloses is the
#   environment's whole resource inventory plus any non-write-only sensitive input an
#   operator supplies.
# Alternatives Considered: the single glob `*.tfstate*`. Rejected because its trailing
#   `*` spans the dot too, so it would also swallow unrelated names such as
#   `x.tfstateignore`. The explicit pair matches the state file and its dot-suffixed
#   variants and nothing else.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# Assumptions: *.tfvars is deliberately NOT ignored, against the usual Terraform
#   convention, because infra/envs/{dev,prod}/terraform.tfvars are required deliverables
#   carrying only sizing, capacity and retention parameters. Automatically loaded
#   override files are a different contract: .github/workflows/deploy.yml generates
#   deployment.auto.tfvars.json under infra/envs/<env>/ from resolved image digests and
#   secret ARNs and deletes it only on its success path, so a failed run leaves it
#   behind. These two narrow suffixes cannot match the tracked deliverables.
*.auto.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars.json

# Local environment overrides for the SPA and the ETL. Real values live only in Secrets
# Manager and Parameter Store and are read at startup.
# Trade-offs: `.env.*` is deliberately broader than the names Vite itself resolves, so
#   it also swallows `.env.bak`, `.env.prod` and any suffix someone invents. Enumerating
#   only `.env.local` and `.env.*.local` leaves a real hole -- Vite resolves
#   `.env.<mode>`, so `ui/.env.production` with a live endpoint in it would be staged by
#   `git add .` with nothing to stop it. Inverting the rule and naming the one tracked
#   file back in is what closes that permanently.
.env
.env.*

# Assumptions: both negations are LOAD-BEARING, not a forward-looking guard. `.env.*`
#   above matches `.env.example` directly, so removing either line would untrack a
#   required deliverable -- ui/.env.example documents variable NAMES with no values. The
#   first covers a template at the repository root, the second one at any depth.
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Python virtual environments. The COBOL parity harness and the data-migration ETL
# share one interpreter created at the repository root.
# Assumptions: data-migration/requirements.txt instructs an operator to create it there
#   and states that these three names are already ignored at any depth, so the rules are
#   a cross-file contract rather than a convenience. Root-anchoring them would break it
#   for an operator who creates an interpreter under data-migration/ instead.
# Trade-offs: `env/` is the broadest of the three and would be a poor rule in a
#   repository that used env/ for configuration. The trailing slash confines it to
#   DIRECTORIES, and infra/envs/ is spelled with a trailing s, so neither is affected.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Saved Terraform plan files, including $root/ecr.tfplan and $root/<env>.tfplan written
# by .github/workflows/deploy.yml, which removes them only when the run succeeds.
# Assumptions: a saved plan is not a diff summary -- it embeds the resolved values of
#   every attribute the apply will set alongside a copy of the prior state, so its
#   exposure is the same as a committed state file's. infra/README.md and
#   infra/bootstrap/README.md each give a `git check-ignore` invocation over *.tfplan
#   that a reader is told to run, so dropping the rule would turn a documented command
#   into a failure.
# Trade-offs: the second pattern is wider than the binary plan alone so that the
#   rendering from `terraform show -json <name>.tfplan` is caught too -- that rendering
#   is easier to read than the binary plan, so protecting only the harder target would
#   miss the easier one. Written as an explicit pair for the same reason as the state
#   rules above.
*.tfplan
*.tfplan.*

# Scratch sources written by the SPA documentation-gate probes in
# ui/src/test/documentationGate.test.ts. Each probe writes one deliberately
# non-conforming module here, lints it through the ESLint Node API against
# ui/eslint.config.js so the assertion measures the REAL gate, and removes the
# directory again in an afterEach that runs whatever the outcome.
# Assumptions: the path has to sit under ui/src, and that is a constraint rather
# than a preference -- ui/eslint.config.js gives the TypeScript parser an
# explicit parserOptions.project list, and ui/tsconfig.json's `include: ["src"]`
# is resolved against the real filesystem, so a probe written anywhere else is
# reported as belonging to no project and the run dies with a parser error before
# a single jsdoc rule is reached.
# Trade-offs: the directory should never exist when git is consulted, so this
# rule protects only against an aborted or interrupted run. It is carried anyway
# because the failure it prevents is a bad one and is easy to misdiagnose: a
# leftover probe under ui/src is by construction non-conforming, so the next
# `npm run lint` fails inside the gate's own package on a file nobody authored.
# Refactoring Rationale: not written as the wider `__*__/` glob. That would also
# swallow a future authored directory using the same dunder convention, and the
# point of naming this one path is that its contents are known to be disposable.
ui/src/__documentation_gate_probe__/
